1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,480
Welcome to this new economic analysis, in which we will explore a truly fascinating question; today's title is exactly this.

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:11,599
Do immigrants make a country richer?

3
00:00:11,599 --> 00:00:19,320
It is a great economic mystery that leads us to wonder if, historically, immigration has actually driven growth,

4
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:24,080
or if people simply moved to where wealth was already present.

5
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:31,240
To find out, we dive into the exceptional research work conducted by Sandra Sequeira, Nathan Nunn, and Nancy Qian.

6
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:32,840
Well, shall we start?

7
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:41,680
To fully understand this dynamic, we need to take a big leap back, into what is called the era of mass migration.

8
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:52,079
Imagine America at that time; as John F. Kennedy brilliantly pointed out, in those decades America became the industrial and agricultural colossus of the planet.

9
00:00:52,079 --> 00:01:02,079
And mind you, all this would never have been possible without the hard work, skills, and intuition of the 23.5 million people who landed during that period.

10
00:01:02,079 --> 00:01:11,400
23.5 million, an unprecedented human wave that literally overturned and shaped the economy, in ways that we are still measuring.

11
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,439
Of course, this phenomenon did not happen overnight.

12
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:23,280
Looking at the timeline, we see that the beginning of this very large-scale migration dates back to the 1850s.

13
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:30,359
But the real turning point, the big shake-up, came in the 1880s, with a massive demographic shift.

14
00:01:30,359 --> 00:01:34,439
It was no longer just people arriving from northern or western Europe,

15
00:01:34,439 --> 00:01:42,920
but huge flows from southern and eastern Europe, an incredible mix of new cultures, languages, and skills pouring into the country.

16
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:51,320
An era that then closed in the 1920s, leaving behind a legacy of a nation with a deeply transformed demography.

17
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:58,359
And here we get to the heart of the matter, to the classic, very difficult chicken-and-egg problem.

18
00:01:58,359 --> 00:02:04,920
Did immigrants choose to settle in counties that were already rich, or was it actually them who made them so?

19
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:08,400
Statistically, this is a real challenge.

20
00:02:08,439 --> 00:02:14,159
If we only base it on raw data, we see counties full of immigrants booming with prosperity.

21
00:02:14,159 --> 00:02:21,640
But there's a catch: maybe they just moved there because new railroads were being built or because industries were already in full boom.

22
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:27,439
Isolating the true cause is quite a puzzle, and that is exactly the heart of this study's analysis.

23
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:32,280
As if that weren't enough, there is a further obstacle, called negative selection.

24
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:43,120
Practically, due to heavy discrimination or simply a lack of resources, the new arrivals often could not buy the best land or access the most lucrative jobs.

25
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,560
They were literally pushed to the margins, into less attractive areas with far fewer prospects.

26
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:59,840
This means that, if we only used basic economic metrics, we could end up dramatically underestimating their real impact, precisely because they started from a position of massive disadvantage.

27
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,200
So, how did the researchers solve the puzzle?

28
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:09,840
With an idea that is a true stroke of methodological genius, they built a natural experiment based on trains.

29
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:14,439
At the time, the railroad was the main way to venture into the interior of the country.

30
00:03:14,439 --> 00:03:22,080
The researchers compared two types of counties: those that were connected to the railway network right during a decade of a migration boom,

31
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:27,759
and those connected during a decade of flat calm, when national arrivals were at historic lows.

32
00:03:27,799 --> 00:03:33,519
Let's get into the details of this tactic; it is an extremely elegant three-step process.

33
00:03:33,519 --> 00:03:40,239
Step one, measure exactly the total flow of immigrants to the United States year by year.

34
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:48,039
Step two, meticulously track the expansion of the railway network, decade by decade.

35
00:03:48,039 --> 00:03:55,319
Step three, and here is the magic, observe where these two pieces of data intersect; timing is everything.

36
00:03:55,439 --> 00:04:01,959
If the railroad arrived in your county just as there was a boom in landings at a national level,

37
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:06,519
you found yourself with a wave of immigrants completely by accident.

38
00:04:06,519 --> 00:04:14,000
This crazy stratagem allows us to isolate the real impact of immigration from any other economic variable.

39
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:23,399
Let's now move on to the long-term economic revelation, measuring prosperity by leaping forward to the year 2000.

40
00:04:23,439 --> 00:04:29,439
Drumroll. Once this brilliant methodology swept away doubts about the causes,

41
00:04:29,439 --> 00:04:34,560
let's see how those counties are doing in the year 2000, more than a century later.

42
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:36,959
The data that emerges is astounding.

43
00:04:36,959 --> 00:04:44,399
The counties that historically received more immigrants today register an average per capita income that is 13% higher.

44
00:04:44,399 --> 00:04:45,879
13%?

45
00:04:45,879 --> 00:04:54,079
Moving a county from zero historical immigration to the 50th percentile makes the current wealth of its inhabitants shoot up by 13%.

46
00:04:54,079 --> 00:04:58,399
A prolonged, solid, and simply huge economic dividend.

47
00:04:58,399 --> 00:05:02,759
But beware, general wealth is not the only positive parameter.

48
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:08,439
Comparing other fundamental indicators of modern well-being, we notice crazy improvements.

49
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:12,879
In those same counties, the poverty rate dropped by two percentage points,

50
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:17,560
and in parallel, the unemployment rate also fell by two percentage points.

51
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:18,920
Think about it for a moment.

52
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:23,759
When you apply decreases of two percentage points to the entire population of a modern county,

53
00:05:23,759 --> 00:05:27,759
we are talking about a massive impact on people's real lives.

54
00:05:27,759 --> 00:05:34,519
And the cascade of benefits continues; the data also reveals a clear increase in the rate of urbanization

55
00:05:34,519 --> 00:05:40,120
which led to the creation of dense, vibrant, and hyper-productive economic centers.

56
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:42,439
But there is something even more fascinating.

57
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:48,519
A significant leap is recorded in the average years of education completed by current residents,

58
00:05:48,519 --> 00:05:53,120
it is as clear as day that that initial impulse brought by the newcomers

59
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,759
did not stop at the gates of the first factories,

60
00:05:55,759 --> 00:05:59,920
but took root in the structural and cultural development of those areas.

61
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:02,560
At this point, the question arises naturally,

62
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:05,319
what are the mechanisms of this success?

63
00:06:05,319 --> 00:06:10,079
In short, how did they manage to generate benefits that last 100 years?

64
00:06:10,199 --> 00:06:13,359
In the short term, the levers of this incredible growth

65
00:06:13,359 --> 00:06:15,439
operated on three main fronts.

66
00:06:15,439 --> 00:06:20,479
On one hand, we had a massive and providential supply of unskilled labor,

67
00:06:20,479 --> 00:06:24,279
a real fuel to power the nascent industrial revolution.

68
00:06:24,279 --> 00:06:28,319
On the other hand, a key group of highly skilled artisans,

69
00:06:28,319 --> 00:06:31,000
who brought specialized crafts from Europe,

70
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,479
and let's not forget the fields.

71
00:06:32,479 --> 00:06:38,000
The agricultural sector took a crazy leap forward thanks to innovations like the Alfalfa Seed

72
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,279
and highly advanced techniques for crop rotation.

73
00:06:41,279 --> 00:06:43,399
But, speaking of innovation,

74
00:06:43,399 --> 00:06:47,040
the level of ingenuity introduced was incredible,

75
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:51,600
many of the most iconic advancements of that era bear the signature of immigrants,

76
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:53,399
think of John A. Roebling,

77
00:06:53,399 --> 00:06:55,480
who pioneered suspension bridges,

78
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,439
like the legendary Brooklyn Bridge,

79
00:06:57,439 --> 00:06:59,279
or Alexander Graham Bell,

80
00:06:59,279 --> 00:07:01,279
to whom we owe the invention of the telephone.

81
00:07:01,279 --> 00:07:03,000
And it's not just engineering,

82
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,680
even civil society was revolutionized,

83
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,160
the idea of preschool, the Kindergarten,

84
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:10,800
was imported by immigrant Friedrich Froebel,

85
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,360
changing early childhood education forever.

86
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:18,480
The increase in patents registered in these areas was literally exponential.

87
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:22,519
There is, however, a fascinating paradox regarding education.

88
00:07:22,519 --> 00:07:27,240
If we look at the short term, in the 19th century the mass arrival of immigrants

89
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:30,680
actually lowered school enrollment rates

90
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:32,800
and caused illiteracy to shoot up,

91
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,480
given that many were escaping rural poverty.

92
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:39,400
However, in the long term, the trend completely reversed.

93
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:42,079
The communities that, at the time, welcomed immigrants,

94
00:07:42,079 --> 00:07:44,600
ended up producing subsequent generations

95
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:48,680
with an average level of education clearly higher than the national average,

96
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,680
an investment in human capital

97
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:53,000
that just needed time to flourish.

98
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,600
Finally, let's examine the social costs and the conclusions

99
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,480
to really get the complete picture of the situation,

100
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:00,960
without leaving anything out.

101
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:03,840
It is more than legitimate to wonder, okay,

102
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:05,920
the economic benefits are huge,

103
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:10,080
but did we pay a price in terms of long-term social tensions?

104
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:12,280
Analyzing the data from the year 2000,

105
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:14,040
the result is crystal clear.

106
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:16,040
Despite the documented hostilities

107
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:19,280
and the undeniable short-term political friction of that era,

108
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:22,600
the long-term impact on things like social capital,

109
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:26,440
voter turnout, and crime rates is, today, zero.

110
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:28,360
No significant effect.

111
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:31,759
In short, the social costs literally melted away

112
00:08:31,759 --> 00:08:36,039
over time, while the economic benefits consolidated.

113
00:08:36,039 --> 00:08:40,919
And this leaves us with a very, very powerful final question.

114
00:08:40,919 --> 00:08:43,480
This empirical analysis shows that short-term

115
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:45,440
tensions tend to fade,

116
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,560
while economic dividends continue

117
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:49,960
to enrich society for more than a century.

118
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:53,440
Faced with such clear evidence, the crucial question is,

119
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:56,720
how should learning this rigorous historical lesson

120
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:00,160
guide and shape our understanding of the dynamics

121
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:02,759
and global movements of the modern economy?

122
00:09:02,759 --> 00:09:06,000
It is a profound reflection that the data invites us not to ignore.

123
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,279
And with this question, I leave you. Until the next analysis.